From 895b770ed0f2ab7ac36f04327f5a570e09102bcf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: unex <7575866+unex@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 08:50:27 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] main: tick slowly while idle, wake for control requests

The main loop slept a fixed 10 ms between ticks. An idle player, with
no stream and nothing on the control socket, so woke the CPU 100 times
a second to find nothing to do. On a small ARM panel (Cortex-A8 at
167 MHz) that alone was about 3.5 % of the CPU.

sendspin-cpp already says when a fast tick matters: it calls
on_request_high_performance() while it plays a stream and while it
runs a time-sync burst, and on_release_high_performance() after. An
ESPHome host speeds up its own loop for the same requests.

Add LoopPacer, a SendspinClientListener that keeps that state, and use
it for the wait: 10 ms (as before) while the library asks for high
performance, 100 ms while it does not. The wait itself is now
ControlSocket::wait(), a poll() on the listening socket and the open
control connections, so a control request ends the wait at once
instead of waiting for the next tick. Without a control socket
(--no-control) it sleeps as before.

Idle, a server message such as stream/start can now wait up to 100 ms
for the next tick. The audio carries its own play-at timestamps, and a
server schedules the first chunk well ahead (aiosendspin 9.1.1: the
client's minimum buffer, 1 s by default), so the late start of the
sink costs no audio. Playback runs at the 10 ms tick as before.

The pacer is declared before the client: the client's destructor
releases what it still holds through the listener.
